  "dom_id": "step-q48728",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037675/assets/d56c10dfa022454135ddafd4255f737182bdca186b99ed1e686132447341bff8.png)\n\nA spherical balloon is being inflated so that its volume is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is changing with respect to the radius at the moment when the diameter of the balloon is equal to $20\\,\\text{cm}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Since we are given that the diameter of the spherical balloon is $20\\,\\text{cm}$,  its radius $r$ is $10\\,\\text{cm}.$ Now, the volume of a sphere with radius $r$  is given by \n\n$$\nV=V(r)=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $V(r)$ with respect to $r$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{\u2032}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi (3{r}^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\pi {r}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $r=10$ gives\n\n$$\n{V}^{\u2032}(10)=4\\pi (10)^{2}=400\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is increasing when the diameter is $20\\,\\text{cm}$ is $400\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{cm}.$",
